{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfUTxXhzHiRTu9d6UTzSy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Quick math first\n\n**Current west-coast volume:** 35% × 4,500/week = 1,575 orders/week ≈ **82,000 orders/year**.\n\n**3PL cost at that volume:** 82,000 × $3.10 ≈ **$254,000/year**, scales linearly, no upfront commitment.\n\n**Warehouse cost:** $420,000 lease + 8 hires. You didn't give me a loaded labor cost, so I'll flag that assumption — using a mid-range fully-loaded warehouse wage (~$50k/head incl. payroll tax/benefits, reasonable for west-coast labor markets): 8 × $50k = $400,000. Total ≈ **$820,000/year fixed**, before software, equipment, utilities, or insurance — so treat this as a floor, not a ceiling.\n\n**Breakeven volume:** $820,000 ÷ $3.10 ≈ **265,000 orders/year** (≈5,100/week west-coast, or the company's *total* weekly volume roughly tripling with the same 35% mix).\n\nAt today's volume, the warehouse's implied cost per order is **~$10/order** — more than 3x the 3PL's price. You'd need west-coast volume to roughly **3.2x** before the warehouse pays for itself on cost grounds alone, and that's before the extra capex/software/insurance costs push the real breakeven even higher.\n\n## What this means for each option\n\n- **Build now:** Locks you into $1.26M of fixed lease cost over 3 years for a volume that doesn't justify it today. You're betting that west-coast growth triples within the lease term — \"growing\" isn't a number, so this bet is unverified. If growth stalls, you're stuck paying ~4x the market rate per order.\n- **3PL now:** Cheaper at current volume by a wide margin, no capital commitment, and delivery SLA (2-day) matches what you'd get from the warehouse. The real cost isn't dollars — it's the packaging/returns control you're trading away, which affects brand experience and return-rate economics in ways not captured in the $3.10 figure.\n- **Split now:** Doesn't make sense yet. Splitting means paying the warehouse's full fixed cost *and* a chunk of 3PL's per-order cost, so you get the worst of both — a fixed cost that's under-utilized and no cost-per-order improvement anywhere. Splitting only earns its keep as a *redundancy* strategy once you're already near warehouse breakeven and want two fulfillment legs for resilience — not as a cost strategy at 82k orders/year.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Go 3PL now**, but don't treat \"less control\" as unfixable — negotiate it into the contract: packaging spec sheet with penalties for non-compliance, return-QC audit rights, and a defined chargeback/credit process for damage or mis-picks. That converts the \"less control\" risk into a monitored SLA rather than a blind spot.\n\nSet an explicit **trigger to revisit building**: once west-coast run-rate approaches ~5,000 orders/week (roughly triple today's volume) sustained for a couple of quarters, redo this math — at that point the warehouse's per-order cost starts beating the 3PL's, and a 3-year lease is a much safer bet against a demonstrated trend rather than a hoped-for one.\n\nOne thing I don't have: your actual west-coast *growth rate*. If you can share it (or recent monthly west-coast order counts), I can tell you roughly how many years out that 3x breakeven is — that's the number that actually decides this, not the current split."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16578,"cache_read_input_tokens":24048,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16578},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"27abcdb4-1b73-4e7c-bc03-4e99b106b77e","uuid":"12c14ebd-1c55-4346-a42e-0f30535e3521","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:05:41.401Z","request_id":"req_011CfUTxX9WSpcYjdwG2uxDu"}
